  html-is-gpu-bridge : Text -> Boolean
  html-is-gpu-bridge (n) =
    text-contains " gpu-open-then gpu-buf-copy-range gpu-noise-bank-then gpu-buf-alloc gpu-buf-free gpu-buf-write-words gpu-buf-download-then gpu-buf-upload-then gpu-buf-show-then gpu-done-then gpu-trim-then clock-ms gpu-launch gpu-error gpu-f32-bits pick-file-then pick-dir-then file-read-then file-url image-pixels-then texture-pixels-then dir-open-then file-write-then file-save-bytes-then clock-utc-text file-delete-then canvas-save-then file-exists-then file-sha256-then audio-analyze-then " (" " & n & " ")

 The GPU bridge (apps/webapp/WebGpu.codex). A handle indexes _gb from 1. A
 launch binds by the WGSL plug's `// cx-kernel <entry> wg=<n> p=bK|uK ...` line:
 bK is storage binding K, uK field K of the uniform at the binding after the
 last storage one, and a scalar arrives as its 32-bit pattern. WebGPU reports a
 validation error asynchronously, so a launch answers 0 once queued and
 gpu-error reads the last error reported. A storage binding's offset must be a
 multiple of 256 (minStorageBufferOffsetAlignment). A dimension holds at most
 65535 workgroups (maxComputeWorkgroupsPerDimension), so a one-dimensional gx
 past that is dispatched as gx by gy, which the WGSL plug's entry linearizes.
 WebGPU frees a destroyed buffer only after the work queued on it runs, so a
 page that queues a whole generation would hold every temporary at once; a
 freed buffer goes to a pool by size instead, and an alloc of that size takes
 it back cleared, which is safe because one queue runs in order.
 Launches and clears are recorded into one pending command encoder, submitted
 every 32 dispatches and before any queue write or readback, because
 queue.writeBuffer runs ahead of every command buffer not yet submitted.

